          "distinct": true,
          "id": "f87b0a6458bdb1de7833e1a4fd24491334b1a6dc",
          "message": "fix(#521): parse integer literals >= 2^63 as BigInt (#539)\n\n* fix(#521): parse integer literals >= 2^63 as BigInt\n\n## Summary\n\nThe lexer was calling `.parse::<i64>()` (and `i64::from_str_radix`) on\nall integer literal tokens and returning a `Lex error: invalid integer`\non overflow.  Decimal, hex, octal, and binary literals larger than\ni64::MAX all failed.\n\n## What Changed\n\n- `crates/pyrust/Cargo.toml`: add `num-bigint = \"0.4\"` as a direct\n  dependency so `lexer.rs` and `compiler.rs` can use `BigInt::parse_bytes`\n  / `BigInt::parse::<BigInt>()` directly.\n- `token.rs`: add `Token::BigInt(String)` — a decimal string\n  representation of the parsed value.  Storing decimal avoids carrying\n  a radix tag through the token/AST pipeline.\n- `lexer.rs` (`lex_number`): after `i64::from_str_radix` / `.parse::<i64>()`\n  fails with overflow, re-parse via `BigInt::parse_bytes` and emit\n  `Token::BigInt(big.to_string())`.  Also accept `_` digit separators in\n  hex/octal/binary literals, which CPython allows and we previously did not.\n- `ast.rs`: add `Expr::BigInt(String)`.\n- `parser.rs`: handle `Token::BigInt` in `parse_primary` and in the\n  pattern-literal arm (including negated BigInt patterns).\n- `compiler.rs`: handle `Expr::BigInt` in all match arms:\n  - `fold_constant` / `try_literal_const_idx` / the main compile dispatch:\n    parse the decimal string back to `PyBigInt` and emit `Value::bigint(n)`.\n  - All leaf arms (no captures, no side effects, invariant, etc.): treat\n    `Expr::BigInt` the same as `Expr::Int`.\n- `interpreter/helpers.rs` (`is_pure_expr`): add `Expr::BigInt` as a\n  pure leaf.\n- New parity fixture: `crates/pyrust/tests/cases/runtime/test_bigint_literal.py`\n  covering decimal, hex, octal, binary large literals, underscore\n  separators, round-trip arithmetic, and boundary values around i64::MAX.\n\n## Self-review caught\n\n- The original lexer did not accept `_` separators in hex/octal/binary\n  literals (e.g. `0xFF_FF`).  The fix passes through the digit-collecting\n  loop, so the separator support fell naturally out of the change.\n  CPython accepts them; behaviour now matches.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- `cargo test --release --bin pyrust` — 284 tests, all green.\n- `cargo test --release --test parity_compare` — new fixture and all\n  existing cases pass.\n- Manual repro: `9999999999999999999999999999999`, `0xDEADBEEFDEADBEEFDEADBEEF`,\n  `0o1000000000000000000000`, `0b1000...0` (64 bits), underscore literals —\n  all match `python3.12` output byte-for-byte.\n\n* fix(#539 review): reject trailing and double underscores in numeric literals\n\nThe PR's underscore-separator changes accepted any underscore position\n(trailing, double) because it simply filtered '_' out before parsing.\nCPython rejects '1_', '1__2', '0xFF_', '0b__1', '0o7_' etc. with\nSyntaxError.\n\nAdd validate_underscores() that walks the raw digit slice and returns\nErr on consecutive or trailing underscores, matching CPython 3.12\nsemantics. Applied to all four bases (decimal, hex, octal, binary) and\nto the imaginary-suffix path. Leading underscores after the base prefix\n(e.g. '0x_FF') remain valid, matching CPython.",
