          "distinct": true,
          "id": "61c5ea8c7d14d19d4d11314a63793d85396b2513",
          "message": "fix(#663): raise TypeError for zero-arg list/tuple index() and count() (#668)\n\n## Summary\n\nBoth `seq_index` and `seq_count` in `crates/pyrust-builtins/src/sequence.rs`\nused `PyError::Runtime(...)` for the zero-argument early-return, producing the\nwrong exception class (`RuntimeError` instead of `TypeError`) and messages that\ndidn't match CPython 3.12.\n\n## What Changed\n\n- `seq_index` zero-arg path: replaced `PyError::Runtime(format!(...))` with\n  `PyError::named(\"TypeError\", \"index expected at least 1 argument, got 0\")`.\n  The message is a static string (no type_name prefix) — matching CPython's\n  exact wording for both `list.index()` and `tuple.index()`.\n\n- `seq_count` zero-arg path: replaced `PyError::Runtime(format!(...))` with\n  `PyError::named(\"TypeError\", format!(\"{type_name}.count() takes exactly one\n  argument (0 given)\"))`. The `type_name` parameter already carries \"list\" or\n  \"tuple\" from the caller, so this naturally produces the correct per-type\n  message.\n\n## Self-review caught\n\nNothing non-obvious. The remaining `PyError::Runtime` calls in `seq_index`\n(non-integer start/stop bounds at lines 13–15 and 23–25) are a different bug\ntracked in #657/#658 and intentionally out of scope here.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- Parity fixture `crates/pyrust/tests/cases/types/test_seq_zero_args.py`\n  covers all four zero-arg cases for both non-empty and empty sequences.\n- `cargo test --release --test parity_compare` passes.",
          "timestamp": "2026-05-19T15:32:08+09:00",
          "tree_id": "e85e19a13840b4295f280fd795ba291859621c1b",
          "url": "https://github.com/ChanyaVRC/pyrust/commit/61c5ea8c7d14d19d4d11314a63793d85396b2513"
