          "id": "901eee488d63620b16ba6d87fec94aaa2983d96a",
          "message": "feat(#649): add math.copysign, isfinite, gcd, factorial, lcm, comb, perm, prod, hypot, dist (#653)\n\n* feat(#649): add math.copysign, isfinite, gcd, factorial, lcm, comb, perm, prod, hypot, dist\n\n## Summary\n\nTen commonly-used math functions were missing from the pyrust math module,\nraising AttributeError where CPython 3.12 returns a result.  All ten are\nnow implemented in `crates/pyrust/src/builtin_modules/bodies/math.rs`\ninside the existing `pyrust_module!` block.\n\n## What Changed\n\n- `math.copysign(x, y)`: two-arg float, `x.copysign(y)`, marked `#[pure]`.\n- `math.isfinite(x)`: single-arg float predicate, `x.is_finite()`, `#[pure]`.\n- `math.hypot(*coords)`: variadic positional floats, sum of squares then sqrt,\n  no keyword args, zero args returns 0.0, `#[pure]`.\n- `math.dist(p, q)`: two equal-length sequences of floats; raises ValueError\n  \"both points must have the same number of dimensions\" on length mismatch, `#[pure]`.\n- `math.gcd(*integers)`: variadic; folds with Euclidean GCD; result always\n  non-negative; accepts bool (subclass of int); math.gcd() → 0, `#[pure]`.\n- `math.lcm(*integers)`: variadic; folds with LCM; math.lcm() → 1, `#[pure]`.\n- `math.factorial(n)`: strict-int only (TypeError for float); ValueError for\n  negative; produces BigInt for n > 20 via iterative BigInt multiply, `#[pure]`.\n- `math.comb(n, k)`: iterative binomial coefficient avoiding full factorial\n  materialisation; k > n returns 0, `#[pure]`.\n- `math.perm(n, k=None)`: P(n,k) iteratively; k defaults to n (full\n  permutation); k > n returns 0, `#[pure]`.\n- `math.prod(iterable, *, start=1)`: collects via `_interp.collect_iterable`,\n  multiplies with `eval_binary(BinaryOp::Mul)`, supports `start` keyword.\n\nAdded helpers:\n- `collect_float_seq`: extracts list/tuple elements as f64 (for dist).\n- `value_to_bigint_int`: Int/BigInt/Bool → PyBigInt, TypeError otherwise.\n- `value_to_bigint_strict_int`: same, used by factorial.\n- `bigint_gcd`, `bigint_lcm`, `bigint_to_int_value`: BigInt arithmetic helpers.\n\nParity fixture: `crates/pyrust/tests/cases/stdlib/test_math_missing.py`\ncovers happy paths and all documented error paths for all ten functions.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- Outputs verified byte-for-byte against CPython 3.12 for all functions,\n  all error paths, and edge cases (negative inputs, k > n, zero args,\n  bool args, big factorial, variadic hypot).\n- `cargo test --release --bin pyrust`: 286/286 passed.\n- `cargo test --release --test parity_compare`: pre-existing failure on\n  list/tuple index error class (RuntimeError vs ValueError) unrelated to\n  this PR; confirmed identical on master.\n\n* fix(#653 review): handle perm(n, None) as perm(n)\n\nCPython 3.12 treats math.perm(n, None) identically to math.perm(n):\nthe second argument's default is None and passing None explicitly means\n\"full permutation\".  The implementer's code only defaulted k to n when\nargs.len() == 1; passing None as a second positional arg fell through to\nvalue_to_bigint_int which raised TypeError.\n\nFix: skip the integer-conversion branch when args[1] is ValueKind::None\nand fall through to the existing `k = n.clone()` default.  Add a\nparity fixture line to lock this in.\n\n* fix(#653 review): address 5 Copilot review comments on math module\n\n- TypeError (not RuntimeError) for all arity checks: single_float helper,\n  pow, atan2, log, copysign, dist, factorial, comb, perm, prod (#5)\n- perm() negative-n error message is now context-sensitive: uses\n  \"factorial() not defined for negative values\" when k is omitted/None\n  (matching CPython perm(-1)), and \"n must be a non-negative integer\"\n  when k is an explicit integer (matching CPython perm(-1, 0)) (#1)\n- comb() and perm() k_u64 conversion changed from unwrap_or(u64::MAX)\n  to ok_or_else OverflowError, preventing a potential infinite loop\n  on astronomically large k values (#2)\n- dist() now accepts any iterable via _interp.collect_iterable(), not\n  just list/tuple; #[pure] removed since collect_iterable can invoke\n  user __iter__ (#4)\n- collect_float_seq removed (dead after dist rewrite); its val.repr()\n  type-name bug therefore eliminated (#3)",
