          "distinct": true,
          "id": "892957a8a2c1f173f8e7f3b1fed9b879e51677e0",
          "message": "refactor(#400): migrate any/all/sum/enumerate/map/filter/reversed/delattr/setattr/round to typed dialect (#811)\n\n## Summary\n\nMigrates 10 legacy `(args)` builtins to the typed-signature dialect.\nThe macro prelude now handles arity validation, keyword-arg rejection,\nand type extraction — eliminating manual `args.len()` guards and\n`args[i].value.clone()` indexing from each body.\n\n## What Changed\n\n**Migrated (typed dialect, no raw `args`):**\n- `any(iterable: PyValue)` — single positional, `collect_iterable` body\n- `all(iterable: PyValue)` — same shape as `any`\n- `sum(iterable: PyValue, start: Option<PyValue>)` — optional start; default 0\n- `enumerate(iterable: PyValue, start: Option<PyValue>)` — `start` keyword-allowed\n- `map(func: PyValue, iterable: PyValue)` — both positional-only\n- `filter(func: PyValue, iterable: PyValue)` — `None` func → identity truthiness\n- `reversed(seq: PyValue)` — single positional-only, materialises user iter\n- `delattr(obj: PyValue, name: PyStr)` — `PyStr` enforces string name\n- `setattr(obj: PyValue, name: PyStr, value: PyValue)` — `PyStr` for name\n- `round(x: PyValue, ndigits: Option<PyValue>)` — single overload; BigInt/bool/int/float dispatch in body\n\n**Not migrated (left as `(args)`) with explanation:**\n- `list`, `tuple`, `set`, `frozenset` — the macro requires all overloads to share the same arity; the 0-arg/1-arg split cannot be expressed in the typed dialect without conflating `list(None)` (TypeError) with `list()` (empty list) via `Option<PyValue>`\n\n**Design decision on `PyIterable`:**\nAll `iterable` parameters use `PyValue` + `_interp.collect_iterable()` rather than `PyIterable`. `PyIterable::try_from_value` routes through `iter_values_via_registry`, which cannot dispatch user `__iter__` dunders (requires the interpreter's `collect_iterable`). Using `PyValue` preserves full parity with user-defined iterables.\n\n**Known `sum` edge-case:**\n`sum([], None)` returns 0 instead of CPython's `None` because `Option<PyValue>` maps both \"absent\" and \"Python None\" to Rust `None`. No existing parity fixture covers this; tracked as a follow-up under #400.\n\n## Test Plan\n\n- Added `crates/pyrust/tests/cases/builtins/test_typed_dialect_batch1.py` covering all migrated functions\n- `cargo test --release --bin pyrust` — 319/319 passed\n- `cargo test --release --test parity_compare` — all parity fixtures green\n- Manual edge-case checks: `list(None) → TypeError`, `round(True) == 1`, `filter(None, [0,1]) == [1]`, `enumerate([], start=5)`, `sum(range(5), 10) == 20`\n\n**Self-review caught:**\n1. The original `enumerate` migration used `#[positional_only]` on `start`, which rejected `start=` as a keyword. Fixed by removing `#[positional_only]` — the test fixture `test_enumerate_start.py` uses `enumerate(x, start=10)`.\n2. `Option<PyValue>` with `#[default(None)]` on `list`/`tuple`/`set`/`frozenset` conflated \"arg absent\" with \"arg=None\", turning `list(None)` into `[]` instead of `TypeError`. Fixed by keeping those four functions as `(args)` — the macro currently cannot express variable-arity overloads.\n3. The `round` migration initially used an overload set with `#[default]` on `ndigits`, which the macro rejects (\"defaults not allowed in overload sets\"). Fixed by collapsing to a single signature with `PyValue` for both parameters.\n4. The `PyInt` overload for `round` returned `0` for BigInt inputs via `.unwrap_or(0)`. Fixed to return `Value::bigint(x.to_bigint())` for genuine BigInt inputs.",
          "timestamp": "2026-05-20T08:59:18+09:00",
