          "distinct": true,
          "id": "0f21cc9628312e8e967fcde7a4388980b511d3c8",
          "message": "perf(#934): short-circuit None-key cross-variant dedup scan in dict/set (#949)\n\n## Summary\n\nPR #929 fixed hash(None) correctness (issue #906) by adding a cross-variant\ndedup check: whenever a PyKey::None key is written to a dict or set, the VM\nscanned for any PyKey::Object entry with hash == py_hash_none() that might\neq-match None.  This scan was unconditional, causing a ~47% regression on\ntight `d[None] = v` loops.\n\n## What Changed\n\nAdd a fast pre-check before the cross-variant scan in all six sites:\n\n- `dict_lookup` (used by GetItem and SetItem via Rc-clone path)\n- `dict_lookup_in` (used by dict_insert and BuildDict)\n- `dict_insert` (used by BuildDict)\n- `set_lookup` (used by SetAdd via Rc-clone path)\n- `set_lookup_in` (used by set_insert)\n- `set_insert` (used by set comprehension building)\n\nAnd in the two VM dispatch sites that gate the entire slow path:\n\n- `Insn::SetItem` dict branch: pre-check before Rc-clone + dict_lookup\n- `Insn::SetAdd` set branch: pre-check before Rc-clone + set_lookup\n\nFor PyKey::None, the pre-check does `dict.keys().any(|k| matches!(k,\nPyKey::Object { hash, .. } if *hash == none_hash))`.  In the common case\n(no cross-variant Object entry exists) this returns false immediately at the\nfirst non-Object key and skips the entire dedup path.  The correctness\nguarantee from issue #906 is fully preserved when cross-variant entries do\nexist.\n\n## Benchmark (from /tmp/ binaries, 5 warmup, 15 runs)\n\n- d[None] = v tight loop (100,000 iterations):\n  - base (origin/master, with PR #929 regression): 9.2 ms\n  - PR:                                            7.4 ms  (-20%)\n- d[\"key\"] = v control (str key, unaffected):\n  - base: 7.5 ms, PR: 7.2 ms  (within noise)\n\n**Self-review caught:**\n- The DelItem handler (line 1712) only calls dict_lookup for PyKey::Object\n  keys and routes None through dict.shift_remove directly — no change needed\n  there.\n- dict_insert calls dict_lookup_in which also has the internal any() check.\n  In the cross-variant collision case this means two any() passes, but that\n  scenario is extremely rare (requires a user object with hash == hash(None)\n  and __eq__(None) == True) so the double scan is immaterial.",
          "timestamp": "2026-05-21T18:37:53+09:00",
