          "distinct": true,
          "id": "9142b4e6ffde9904518e9602b2838e9b2ac87426",
          "message": "perf(#345): adaptive BinOp inline cache for float/str fast paths (#962)\n\n* perf(#345): adaptive BinOp inline cache for float/str fast paths\n\n## Summary\n\nImplements a PEP 659-style adaptive inline cache for BinOp instructions.\nEach `FnCode` now carries a `binop_cache: RefCell<Vec<BinOpCacheEntry>>`\n(indexed by `pc`, same pattern as the existing `attr_cache`), which\ntransitions through `Empty` → `Counting` → `Specialized` → `Megamorphic`\nbased on observed operand types.\n\n## What Changed\n\n- `bytecode.rs`: Added `BinopTypeTag`, `BinOpCacheEntry`, `BINOP_SPEC_THRESHOLD`\n  and `binop_cache` field on `FnCode`.\n- `compiler.rs` / `optimizer.rs`: Initialize `binop_cache` alongside\n  `attr_cache` at FnCode construction.\n- `vm.rs`:\n  - New `float_float_fast()` fast-path evaluator for `(f64, f64)` ops.\n    Guards `Div`, `FloorDiv`, `Mod` for zero-divisor and `Pow` for\n    `0.0 ** negative` by returning `None` (falls through to `eval_binary`\n    which raises the correct `ZeroDivisionError`).\n  - New `str_str_fast()` fast-path evaluator for `(str, str)` ops\n    (Add = concat, and comparison operators).\n  - `classify_binop_tag()` maps a value pair to `BinopTypeTag`.\n  - `Insn::BinOp` dispatch: the existing unconditional int-int fast path\n    (`int_int_fast`) runs first with zero cache overhead. After it misses,\n    the cache is consulted. `Specialized(Float)` and `Specialized(Str)` hit\n    their respective fast paths on type match. Deopt to `Megamorphic` only\n    on type mismatch, not on edge-case values (div-by-zero stays Specialized).\n  - Test `empty_code` helper updated to include `binop_cache`.\n\n## Self-review caught\n\n- **ZeroDivisionError correctness**: Rust `1.0_f64 / 0.0` returns `inf`\n  rather than panicking. The initial `float_float_fast` omitted the zero\n  guard for `Div`, which would have silently returned `inf` instead of\n  raising `ZeroDivisionError` when the float specialization was active.\n  Fixed by returning `None` when divisor is 0 for `Div`, `FloorDiv`, `Mod`;\n  and when `a == 0.0 && b < 0.0` for `Pow`. Verified with a parity fixture\n  that exercises these paths after the cache reaches `Specialized(Float)`.\n- **Deopt policy**: Changed from \"deopt on any None from fast path\" to\n  \"deopt only on type mismatch\". This keeps float/str sites Specialized\n  even when edge cases occasionally fall through to `eval_binary`.\n\n## Benchmark (from /tmp/ binaries, WSL)\n\nfloat-heavy loop (`total += 1.5`, 1M iterations):\n  master: 39.1 ms  PR: 36.8 ms  → 1.06× speedup\nint-heavy loop (`x = x + i`, 1M iterations):\n  master: 12.6 ms  PR: 12.7 ms  → no regression (within noise)\nstr comparison loop (100k iterations):\n  master: 14.3 ms  PR: 14.1 ms  → marginal improvement\n\n* fix(#962 review): correct BinOpCacheEntry doc — only BinOp uses the cache\n\nThe doc on BinOpCacheEntry and FnCode::binop_cache claimed that\nBinOpInPlace, BinOpConst, and BinOpImm also advance the cache past Empty.\nIn the implementation only Insn::BinOp wires the adaptive cache; the\nother three variants use only the unconditional int-int fast path.\nCorrected both doc-comments to match the implementation.",
