          "distinct": true,
          "id": "2d4686eac2c68df7d2de217b450f6f45f4cf3fba",
          "message": "fix(#970): locals() at module scope returns live module dict (not a snapshot) (#971)\n\n## Summary\n\nAt module scope, `locals()` already returned the same `module_globals_dict`\nobject as `globals()`, so `locals() is globals()` was already `True`. However,\nwriting through the returned dict (e.g. `locals()[\"x\"] = 99`) did not propagate\nto the fastlocal register for `x`. Because module-scope variable reads\n(compiled as `regs[slot]`) go directly to the fastlocal register, the write\nwas silently lost and `print(x)` still printed the old value.\n\n## What Changed\n\n`Insn::SetItem` (dict branch): after inserting a key-value pair into a dict,\ncheck whether that dict is `module_globals_dict` (via `Rc::ptr_eq` on the\ninner `Rc<RefCell<IndexMap>>`). If it is, and the key is a string name that has\na fastlocal register slot in the current script frame, write the new value into\nthat register. Also bump `global_env_version` to invalidate any stale\n`LoadGlobal` inline-cache entries for the name.\n\n`Insn::DeleteItem` (dict branch): same detection; on a match, clear the\nfastlocal register to `Value::unset()` so that a subsequent direct register\nread correctly raises a NameError rather than returning the stale value.\n\nBoth changes are gated on `self.globals_accessed` (false until `globals()` or\n`locals()` is first called), so scripts that never call either function pay\nzero overhead — only the Rc::ptr_eq check is added to the dict-SetItem/\nDeleteItem path, and it short-circuits immediately when the flag is clear.\n\n## Self-review caught\n\nThe `del locs[\"x\"]` case was not part of the original issue description but\nis a natural sibling: clearing the fastlocal register on dict-delete ensures\nthat `print(x)` after `del locals()[\"x\"]` raises rather than returning stale\ndata. Fixed in the same patch.\n\nThe deletion NameError message differs from CPython (`NameError: name 'x' is\nnot defined` vs. pyrust's `UnboundLocalError: cannot access local variable...`\nfor the `CheckLocal` path). This is a pre-existing mismatch in how module-scope\nfastlocals interact with the error-reporting machinery; left as a separate\nfollow-up.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- `locals() is globals()` at module scope → `True` (was already True)\n- `locals()[\"x\"] = 99; print(x)` → `99` (was `1`)\n- `locals()[\"x\"] = 99` inside a function does NOT affect the local `x` (snapshot\n  semantics preserved — no `globals_accessed` dict Rc match for function frames)\n- `globals()[\"y\"]` still works correctly\n- All 385 unit tests pass; parity_compare (40 s) passes\n- New fixture: `crates/pyrust/tests/cases/runtime/test_locals_module.py`",
          "timestamp": "2026-05-25T19:44:03+09:00",
          "tree_id": "343a97b508eb2094c2aeb8b1b21413b4a0298d77",
