          "distinct": true,
          "id": "2782e1b614e721d7a2b7e7e7f3562f1118c1ddc8",
          "message": "fix(#972): del d[missing] raises KeyError (#973)\n\n## Summary\n\n`del d[key]` on a dict silently discarded the missing-key case. CPython\n3.12 raises `KeyError` when the key is absent.\n\n## What Changed\n\n`Insn::DeleteItem` in `vm.rs` has two dict code paths:\n\n- Object keys (user `__hash__`/`__eq__`): `dict_lookup` returned\n  `Option<(idx, _)>` and the `None` branch fell through without an\n  error. Added an `else` arm that raises `PyError::key_error(idx_val)`.\n\n- Non-object keys (int, str, float, etc.): `dict_with_mut(|dict|\n  dict.shift_remove(&key))` returns `Option<Option<Value>>` — the inner\n  `None` signals a missing key. The return value was discarded; now we\n  check `!matches!(removed, Some(Some(_)))` and raise `KeyError` when\n  absent.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- New parity fixture: `crates/pyrust/tests/cases/runtime/test_dict_delete.py`\n  exercises normal deletion, missing string key, int key deletion, and\n  missing int key — output matches CPython 3.12 byte-for-byte.\n- `cargo test --release --bin pyrust` — 386 passed, 0 failed.\n- `cargo test --release --test parity_compare` — 1 passed, 0 failed.",
          "timestamp": "2026-05-25T19:58:24+09:00",
